WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.467
LoRA.

2
00:00:01.767 --> 00:00:04.229
The fine-tuning workhorse of the industry.

3
00:00:04.229 --> 00:00:06.633
If you build one thing twice, build this.

4
00:00:07.833 --> 00:00:08.933
Here is the idea.

5
00:00:09.233 --> 00:00:11.200
Freeze all of the original weights.

6
00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:16.300
Then train only tiny low-rank matrices that sit beside the attention layers.

7
00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:22.667
Instead of updating the full d by d matrix W, LoRA learns two thin matrices.

8
00:00:22.967 --> 00:00:24.400
W stays frozen.

9
00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:26.900
B is d by r, where r is small.

10
00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:28.071
A is r by d.

11
00:00:28.071 --> 00:00:31.267
Together they hold far fewer numbers than W.

12
00:00:32.467 --> 00:00:33.800
The layer computes this.

13
00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:37.067
The original path, W times x, is frozen.

14
00:00:37.367 --> 00:00:40.833
Beside it runs the learned bypass, B times A times x.

15
00:00:41.133 --> 00:00:44.417
A scale of alpha over r controls its strength.

16
00:00:44.417 --> 00:00:48.700
At inference you can merge it into W for zero added latency.

17
00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:52.000
Here it is with the PEFT library.

18
00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:55.567
We pick rank eight and target two attention projections.

19
00:00:55.867 --> 00:01:00.400
Printing trainable parameters shows only about point two two percent are trained.

20
00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:03.267
The saved adapter is just a few megabytes.

21
00:01:04.467 --> 00:01:06.733
That small artifact is the superpower.

22
00:01:07.033 --> 00:01:12.084
Mission Control serves two programs from one 7B base, with one adapter each.

23
00:01:12.084 --> 00:01:14.344
Two adapters cost forty megabytes.

24
00:01:14.344 --> 00:01:17.667
Two full models would cost twenty eight gigabytes.

25
00:01:18.867 --> 00:01:22.967
This is the interview classic: how would you serve per customer fine-tunes?

26
00:01:23.267 --> 00:01:25.900
Keep one base model in GPU memory.

27
00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:29.867
Train an adapter for each program, Artemis and heliophysics.

28
00:01:30.167 --> 00:01:32.833
And let the API pick the adapter per request.

29
00:01:34.033 --> 00:01:34.953
Let's recap.

30
00:01:34.953 --> 00:01:38.633
LoRA freezes W and trains thin matrices A and B.

31
00:01:38.933 --> 00:01:42.433
About one percent of the parameters gives most of the benefit.

32
00:01:42.733 --> 00:01:44.633
Adapters are tiny and swappable.

33
00:01:44.933 --> 00:01:47.721
And you can merge them for zero latency.

34
00:01:47.721 --> 00:01:48.767
Next up: QLoRA.
